    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d8">

                <pb facs="0021" n="14" xml:id="pg14"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13940: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-01-22T19:00:00+08:00">January 22, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 22—7:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>34. According to today’s <hi rendition="#italic">North China Star</hi>, General
                    Sung Che Yuan<anchor n="24" xml:id="d8-f1"/> issued an order at Tientsin
                    yesterday to various military and administrative offices in Hopei and Chahar
                    directing that they (1) refrain from internal warfare, (2) resist all foreign
                    aggression, and (3) annihilate all Communists and bandits. Report states Sung
                    informed subordinates that suppression of bandits and Communists is not to be
                    regarded as internal warfare. In a separate manifesto to soldiers of the 29th
                    Army General Sung admonishes them to (1) support the unification of the country
                    and carry out the orders of the Central Government, (2) safeguard the three
                    important attributes of a sovereign state, namely, sovereignty, land and the
                    people, (3) exterminate Communism.</p>
                <p>2. No broadcasts from Sian have been heard for 4 or 5 nights. Information of
                    value concerning the exact military position in Sian, Kansu, and the exact
                    relationship between Yang Hu Cheng and Yu Hsueh Chung on the one side and the
                    Communist armies on the other is extremely hard to obtain in Peiping. Many hold
                    to the belief that the situation may yet be settled by negotiation and that no
                    major military operations will occur. The trend in China definitely is in the
                    direction of doing away with internal warfare as far as possible.</p>
                <p>By mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
